#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147149 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "39259a31-5d67-5482-b1bb-4fa719ce4f95" :question/math-academy-id "e-20894" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{y}{ x^2 } \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $e^{yx}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}.$\n\n First, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}$ to each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}$ above:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ then we get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cbd51b4-cbd1-5e18-8c49-44be7967cd38" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exact Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "ca703f4f-5363-5fc1-8372-136e7c87156d" :question/math-academy-id "e-20895" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = y^2-2x$ and $Q(x,y) = 2xy+1.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int {y}^{2}-2x\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$.  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int 2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147152 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/id #uuid "36440ae0-1cee-52c0-b24a-7871e172a341" :question/math-academy-id "e-20933" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147153 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bb08d6e-ae86-50f5-8cb4-d7fe50595849" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging an ODE Into Exact Form and Then Solving"}] :question/id #uuid "c1b768cb-8709-55f0-878b-d9608ac1c478" :question/math-academy-id "e-20934" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{11+y{e}^{xy}}{13y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(11+y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-13y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (11+y{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =11x+{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad  f(y) = - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ea3880-97d6-5695-959f-c6691bd2a8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340198} {:answer/id #uuid "8418349a-7031-59ca-9349-9aa519acc2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340199} {:answer/id #uuid "8670f68b-2cbd-50cb-a4c3-411f8bf23928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340200} {:answer/id #uuid "9de6bd51-13cf-58cd-806d-a3d3e528aa7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340201} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5fcd89-355f-5aea-8bf6-bead093bda0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47ea3880-97d6-5695-959f-c6691bd2a8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340198} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f23bfff-97f3-5e5d-beb8-38983083c3ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0e84f03-1d6a-5d04-a139-0cf3f851d753" :question/math-academy-id "q-103172" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$\n- $3{y}^{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2})=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$ gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3454ba12-5cb8-5dc0-88cf-7685227e3a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340205} {:answer/id #uuid "37d56f4f-789e-5f7d-91ce-c6c312c7c28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340206} {:answer/id #uuid "52cedde0-0bbf-5e75-9b9e-1a91c171b12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340207} {:answer/id #uuid "980a631b-e920-5150-bd25-a05d11769f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340208} {:answer/id #uuid "c97a74aa-f9ed-59b5-bc0d-a025f921e91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c97a74aa-f9ed-59b5-bc0d-a025f921e91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340209} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b757bc7-03fd-5243-90e7-459c930e796f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5f4a045-5343-5261-852f-034babd8107f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103174" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $6x{y}^{2}$\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$\n- $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{2})=6{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''II only''."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69da71c6-0818-56c1-be1b-d88e3521bc19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340212} {:answer/id #uuid "f68954f9-c3be-586c-9ea2-344b3c3e73d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340213} {:answer/id #uuid "afc1c2f7-83af-587a-acf0-7e2f5c77ab81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340214} {:answer/id #uuid "b6aa73c0-6eb9-5adb-89f6-5b0d4cbbc7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340215} {:answer/id #uuid "73d38f31-1e4d-5a7b-85ee-854c352ef12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afc1c2f7-83af-587a-acf0-7e2f5c77ab81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340214} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d81e09e-2d7a-590b-af64-82f21f2a36ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "368064cc-9b0a-544e-bc34-335b8aa95f98" :question/math-academy-id "q-103175" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$\n- $-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''I only''."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cbd51b4-cbd1-5e18-8c49-44be7967cd38" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exact Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14466c2d-4181-5d2a-baeb-43f48fc28e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340219} {:answer/id #uuid "8d14d9d4-ed81-53f0-9bf7-297b72faf2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340220} {:answer/id #uuid "ce27ece8-be33-5639-a6af-9cba4f8c3599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340221} {:answer/id #uuid "c6945420-0d23-5961-903f-42baee96a523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340222} {:answer/id #uuid "10339ce0-c7f3-5f0c-9725-0455f672610c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d14d9d4-ed81-53f0-9bf7-297b72faf2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340220} :answer-field/id #uuid "d837d48f-c18e-5cbf-8d52-241827191488" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7949606-3a7a-5b98-8e86-415afaf09dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103339" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x+(6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(1)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=0,\\,f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=0$ when $x=1.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=c \\\\ 3\\cdot (1)\\cdot (0)^{2}-2\\cdot (1)^{3}\\cdot (0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=c \\\\ 0+0+1=c \\\\ c=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cbd51b4-cbd1-5e18-8c49-44be7967cd38" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exact Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da460d15-c407-50eb-85bf-be68a009abc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340226} {:answer/id #uuid "090c95eb-400e-5640-8e37-35a80ef250e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340227} {:answer/id #uuid "e67894ba-728a-5bea-8e2a-5af1a80247a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340228} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d7d1a2-a14f-540e-884c-681bf42c5096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340229} {:answer/id #uuid "26d8eda3-5972-54b2-b2e4-42cd31190752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e67894ba-728a-5bea-8e2a-5af1a80247a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340228} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ca6fb9-3fc5-51de-8620-5dbf05bb13ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2d85391-9596-5c36-a133-a6a5b4d8cd71" :question/math-academy-id "q-103341" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{y})\\text{d}y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{y})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,f(y)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a3a581-694d-5109-9da6-450c1d1411f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂r}=-\\frac{∂Q}{∂s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340233} {:answer/id #uuid "7f922dbb-0320-5b0c-ae61-0808e5295f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340234} {:answer/id #uuid "7055ca9f-ac98-5cbc-a77f-c678def961d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{∂}^{2}P}{∂{s}^{2}}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}Q}{∂{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340235} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc5269e-05e9-50f4-8dec-812e6982b076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂r}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f922dbb-0320-5b0c-ae61-0808e5295f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340234} :answer-field/id #uuid "59cd9242-e478-590f-9527-30834986875c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340232} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f600d4-3bce-5515-bad7-59fdafcbd622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-s{e}^{rs}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340238} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2c018e-249c-556b-b9cd-4b3087d7af58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-r{e}^{rs}+4s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340239} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c828d7-2812-5056-879a-ad4a3d2a738a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-rs{e}^{rs}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340240} {:answer/id #uuid "50839768-3f83-567a-95f5-ae1cdfd5968d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{rs}+rs{e}^{rs}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f600d4-3bce-5515-bad7-59fdafcbd622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-s{e}^{rs}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340238} :answer-field/id #uuid "b397dd9b-5413-54d1-8de0-42f770966dab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340237} {:answer