12e746656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is exact" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340259} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2cff2fa-18ec-55ff-8eb6-d55460e81bd4" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6db88e5d-0b4c-539e-9588-685be1e9680f" :question/math-academy-id "q-318601" :question/problem "Determine whether the following differential equation is exact:\n\n$$\n(1-s{e}^{rs})\\,\\text{d}r+(-r{e}^{rs}+4s)\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$\n\nA differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(r,s)\\,\\text{d}r+Q(r,s)\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it satisfies the condition\n\n$$\n\\,{{field-1}},\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(1-s{e}^{rs})\\,\\text{d}r+(-r{e}^{rs}+4s)\\,\\text{d}s=0$, we have  \n\n$$\nP=\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\n$$\nQ=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\,{{field-4}},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂r}=\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\,{{field-6}},\n$$\n\n the equation {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(r,s)\\,\\text{d}r+Q(r,s)\\,\\text{d}s=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂r}.\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(1-s{e}^{rs})\\,\\text{d}r+(-r{e}^{rs}+4s)\\,\\text{d}s=0$, we have  \n\n$$\nP(r,s)=1-s{e}^{rs},\\,Q(r,s)=-r{e}^{rs}+4s.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂}{∂s}(1-s{e}^{rs}) \\\\  & =-\\,({e}^{rs}+rs{e}^{rs}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂}{∂r}(-r{e}^{rs}+4s) \\\\  & =-\\,({e}^{rs}+rs{e}^{rs}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂s}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂r},\n$$\n\nthe exactness condition is satisfied. Therefore, the given differential equation $\\text{is exact}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1c208e-1795-599f-a7c0-ba461be4a7f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340263} {:answer/id #uuid "4c49047e-e96a-56a2-b620-1e257a622a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340264} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4ca63b-e7c1-5af8-82c1-bca687da48fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340265} {:answer/id #uuid "43e766ba-1977-5f22-b198-6205741239e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340266} {:answer/id #uuid "79b13e61-3f09-5a7e-99bb-eac48251f987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c49047e-e96a-56a2-b620-1e257a622a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340264} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d0b19d8-cbca-5872-8304-630cd1975f10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f5d0a5b-358b-5167-8c1b-732bb9d009dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-318630" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy+5,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+5) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+7) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{2}+x,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-2x)=2x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y-3x) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation III is not exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbf848d-8f5b-51fb-803f-1b4165461b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340270} {:answer/id #uuid "cd20f3b3-c7e1-5ff9-ba51-fb345ecc31b